<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Cypress's privacy manifest (task #188, split out of #187).

	  ── NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty, and that is a measured claim ─────────────────────────
	  Apple's "collect" means transmit off the device. Nothing this app holds is transmitted
	  anywhere. `RemoteAPI` implements `CypressAPI` but is a stub and is never instantiated in
	  shipping code — `grep -rn "RemoteAPI(" Cypress` returns nothing, and the outbox drains through
	  `APIOutboxTransport` into `LocalAPI`, which writes this phone's own SQLite. The only
	  `URLSession` traffic in the app is `CityDownloader`, which GETs published city files from a
	  static bucket and uploads nothing at all: no request body, no identifiers, no query built from
	  anything the reader did.

	  So photographs, precise location, check-ins, measurements, notes and the whole contribution
	  history stay on the phone. The app *uses* location and the camera — that is what the three
	  usage strings in Info.plist are for — but using is not collecting, and this array must stay
	  empty only for as long as that remains true.

	  **This entry has a shelf life.** #158 is the contribution sync endpoint and it is unbuilt. The
	  day anything uploads a contribution, this file is wrong, and it is wrong silently: nothing in
	  the build fails, and the first symptom is an App Review rejection or, worse, a shipped app
	  whose label misdescribes it. Whoever builds #158 owns this file in the same commit.

	  ── NSPrivacyTracking ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	  False, with no tracking domains. There is no analytics SDK, no advertising identifier, and no
	  third-party dependency of any kind — the project has zero external dependencies by policy
	  (ARCHITECTURE), so there is no vendor manifest to merge with this one.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<!--
	  ── Required-reason APIs: User Defaults only ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	  Two shipping call sites read and write `UserDefaults.standard`, both storing a fact about this
	  installation's own UI state and nothing else:

	    · `VisitSaveLedger`   — how many times the account ask has been shown (a funnel fact, which is
	                            why it is UserDefaults and not `app_state`; see its header).
	    · `MapOpeningCamera`  — the camera the map last rested at, so a cold launch opens where the
	                            reader left off rather than on a hardcoded city.

	  Reason `CA92.1`: information accessible only to this app, not written by or readable to any
	  other app or the system. Cypress has no app group and no extension, so `1C8F.1` does not
	  apply.

	  The other three required-reason families are absent, checked rather than assumed: no file
	  timestamp API (`creationDate`, `modificationDate`, `contentModificationDate`), no disk space
	  API (`systemFreeSize`, `volumeAvailableCapacity`), and no system boot time API
	  (`systemUptime`, `mach_absolute_time`) appears anywhere under `Cypress/`. Re-run those greps
	  before adding a category here rather than adding one defensively — a declared reason the app
	  cannot justify is its own rejection.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
